# labb's settings. Copy this file to .env and change what you need:
#   cp .env.example .env
# docker compose reads it for the server. A checkout run with `uv run` reads it too, and an
# installed labb reads ~/.labb/.env (install.sh writes that one for you).
# docs/guide.md#settings has every setting.

# --- The model -------------------------------------------------------------------------
# "<provider>:<model>". Pull an Ollama model on the Ollama machine first: ollama pull gemma4:e4b
MODEL=ollama:gemma4:e4b
# For other providers, e.g. MODEL=openai:gpt-5.5 or MODEL=anthropic:claude-sonnet-5-5
# OPENAI_API_KEY=
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# Ollama Cloud (ollama.com → Settings → Keys); without one, cloud models run through a signed-in Ollama server
# OLLAMA_API_KEY=
# With a key, the model picker offers that provider's chat models. To offer only some (on a
# server, admins can change all of this in Settings → Models instead):
# MODELS=openai:gpt-5.5,anthropic:claude-sonnet-5-5

# --- Ollama ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# From the Docker container, Ollama on the same machine is at host.docker.internal.
# Running from a checkout instead, use http://localhost:11434 (or the Ollama machine's address).
OLLAMA_HOST=http://host.docker.internal:11434
# The context window asked of Ollama; its own default is too small for long pages
# OLLAMA_NUM_CTX=32768
# Strict tool calls (the provider keeps arguments to the tools' schemas): auto = OpenAI and
# Anthropic, on = also OpenAI-compatible servers (vLLM 0.31+, LM Studio…), off = never.
# The server's Settings → Models can set it per provider.
# STRICT_TOOLS=auto

# --- Documents and memories (server) ---------------------------------------------------
# The Ollama model that embeds documents and memories (ollama pull embeddinggemma), until an admin
# picks one in Settings → Models → Search; changing it embeds everything again in the background
# EMBED_MODEL=embeddinggemma

# --- Speech (server) --------------------------------------------------------------------
# Settings → Models → Speech picks these on the server; these are the defaults until it does.
# docker compose runs speaches (see compose.yaml) and uses it: faster-whisper-small to listen,
# Kokoro with the voice af_heart to speak. "<provider>:<model>" of OpenAI or an OpenAI-compatible
# provider; for OpenAI's, set the voice too (Kokoro's voices aren't OpenAI's):
# SPEECH_TO_TEXT_MODEL=openai:gpt-4o-transcribe
# TEXT_TO_SPEECH_MODEL=openai:gpt-4o-mini-tts
# TTS_VOICE=alloy
# Where speaches is (compose: http://speaches:8000/v1); without it, there's no speaches provider
# SPEACHES_URL=http://localhost:8001/v1

# --- Web search and reading pages ------------------------------------------------------
# docker compose runs labb-web-search-crawler (crawler/) next to the server and points labb at
# it. Running from a checkout, start it (cd crawler && uv run labb-web-search-crawler) and set:
# CRAWLER_URL=http://localhost:3000
# The browser service (browser/), for the browser tools; in Docker, compose sets it
# BROWSER_URL=http://localhost:3100
# Without one, labb searches DuckDuckGo itself and reads pages as plain text.

# --- The web server --------------------------------------------------------------------
# The port docker compose publishes (and the server listens on outside Docker)
# PORT=8000
# How many scheduled tasks run at once; Ollama answers one request at a time unless OLLAMA_NUM_PARALLEL is raised
# TASK_WORKERS=1
# --- Studio (server) -------------------------------------------------------------------
# docker compose runs the sandbox for Python steps, which makes its own token on the first start
# SANDBOX_TOKEN=
# Secrets for HTTP actions are encrypted with a key kept in the data volume (secret.key),
# or with this; keep it with your backups
# LABB_SECRET_KEY=
# How many workflow runs go at once
# WORKFLOW_WORKERS=2

# --- Apps (server) ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# docker compose runs the spawner, which starts each app as a container of its own and makes its
# own token on the first start. Each app's container is published on a port of its own
# (http://labb.lan:9100, ...; with https, Caddy has them): let these through the server's firewall.
# SPAWNER_TOKEN=
# APP_PORT_FIRST=9100
# APP_PORT_LAST=9199
# 127.0.0.1 to open apps from this machine only
# APP_BIND=0.0.0.0
# Stop apps nobody used for this many minutes (their container's traffic); opening one starts it again
# APP_IDLE_MINUTES=0
# Apps on this server in all (0: as many as there are ports)
# APP_MAX_TOTAL=0
# How much an app may keep in /data (MB); one that grows past it is stopped
# APP_DATA_MB=1024
# labb's address as apps see it, for the apps that may use labb
# APPS_LABB_URL=http://labb-web:8000
# How big an app's database may get (MB); one past it is stopped
# APP_DB_MB=512

# --- Integrations (server) -------------------------------------------------------------
# People connect their own accounts in Settings → Integrations; an admin sets up each OAuth
# client in the server's Settings → Integrations, or here. docs/guide.md#integrations has the steps.
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
# MICROSOFT_CLIENT_ID=
# MICROSOFT_CLIENT_SECRET=
# MICROSOFT_TENANT=common
# GITHUB_CLIENT_ID=
# GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET=
# NOTION_CLIENT_ID=
# NOTION_CLIENT_SECRET=
# The server's address as people reach it, when it's https on a public domain (a reverse
# proxy, Tailscale…): Google sends them back there. Without one, they paste an address.
# LABB_PUBLIC_URL=https://labb.example.com

# Where chats, accounts and the rest are kept. docker compose runs Postgres for the server
# (in the postgres-data volume); set a password for it before the first start (./setup.sh does):
# POSTGRES_PASSWORD=
# Without LABB_DATABASE_URL (a checkout run with `uv run`), they're in a SQLite file, CHAT_DB:
# LABB_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://labb:password@localhost:5432/labb
# CHAT_DB=chats.sqlite

# --- Sites -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# docker compose runs labb-sites: static websites at http://<this machine>:8300/artifacts/<name>/, each
# uploaded as one zip or tar (sites/README.md). The token for uploads, else the one it makes:
#   docker compose exec sites-deploy cat /run/labb-sites/token
# SITES_TOKEN=
# SITES_PORT=8300
# 127.0.0.1 to serve them to this machine only (./setup.sh https sets it; Caddy has SITES_HTTPS_PORT)
# SITES_BIND=0.0.0.0
# SITES_HTTPS_PORT=8301
# Largest archive (and site unpacked), in MB, and files per site
# SITES_MAX_MB=100
# SITES_MAX_FILES=10000
# The web pages labb builds in chats are published there too, and opened at this server's name on
# SITES_PORT; or somewhere else people reach it:
# SITES_PUBLIC_URL=https://pages.example.com
# Web pages per person
# ARTIFACT_MAX=50

# --- Backups (docker compose --profile backup up -d) ------------------------------------
# Every night at BACKUP_AT (in TZ): the database and secret.key in BACKUP_DIR, the newest BACKUP_KEEP kept
# BACKUP_AT=03:00
# BACKUP_KEEP=7
# BACKUP_DIR=./backups
# BACKUP_OWNER=1000:1000
# TZ=Europe/Berlin
# Encrypt each backup (they hold secret.key) with an age key: age-keygen -o labb-backup.key, keep that
# file somewhere else, and put the public key it prints here
# BACKUP_AGE_RECIPIENT=age1...

# --- Development (a checkout) ------------------------------------------------------------
# ./setup.sh dev sets this: labb's images built from the source (compose.dev.yaml) instead of pulled
# COMPOSE_FILE=compose.yaml:compose.dev.yaml
# Which release's images to pull (a release's compose.yaml has its own version as the default)
# LABB_VERSION=0.33.0

# --- HTTPS (optional) -------------------------------------------------------------------
# ./setup.sh https (your network) or ./setup.sh domain (a public domain) sets these; see
# docs/guide.md, HTTPS.
# COMPOSE_PROFILES=https
# LABB_HOSTNAME="labb.lan, 192.168.1.5"
# LABB_DEFAULT_SNI=192.168.1.5
# LABB_TLS=internal
# LABB_BIND=127.0.0.1
# SITES_BIND=127.0.0.1
# FORWARDED_ALLOW_IPS="*"
# LABB_PUBLIC_URL=https://labb.lan
